      "side": 1,
      "message": "I don\u0027t think this really fixes the issue if we spawn the tokio thread in every callback call.\n\nAt the point where a thread starts awaiting (line #780), the other threads are able to lock, pop, and await the next future, then we are falling back to the same problem.\n\nTo fix the problem I think we may either:\n1. Spawn at most one thread and only it can pop the cb_futures\n2. Add an \"awaiting\" lock to indicate someone is popping/awaiting a future, while it still allows the main thread to insert new futures\n\nPersonally I prefer (1) because it saves redundant spawns. But then I found it may need many changes*. So probably (2) is a better fix for now. WDYT?\n\n*To ensure we have at most one thread, the first idea that comes to my mind is to spawn a thread only when the size of cb_futures \u003d\u003d 1. However there is still an edge case because some other thread may just emptied the cb_futures and haven\u0027t yet finished the task. Thus, we still need a lock/semaphore to indicate whether there is an unfinished thread. Otherwise, we need a messages/tasks dispatching system + a forever running thread for executing futures.",
